WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.532
- [Announcer] Three, two, one, zero.

00:00:03.532 --> 00:00:04.521
Ignition.

00:00:04.521 --> 00:00:05.810
(rocket booming)

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.500
- [Narrator] SpaceX is ramping up the pace

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.750
of rocket launches in order

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.010
to conquer the internet from space.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.330
The company has launched
more than 1,000 satellites

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.760
to build a network called Starlink aimed

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.170
at bringing faster connections

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.050
to billions of people.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.995
Meanwhile, China has launched
only a few test satellites.

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:23.100
(rocket booming)

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.090
But the country has sent

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.800
what it calls the world's
first 6G satellite,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.940
which it says would provide faster service

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.230
than what's available on Earth.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.850
- Satellite internet is really seen

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.640
to be and extension of a lot

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.330
of the terrestrial internet services.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.610
- [Narrator] The satellite internet market

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.460
is expected to be worth more
than $400 billion by 2040.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.703
- The world seems

00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:44.630
to have an insatiable
appetite for bandwidth.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.070
- [Narrator] And this
competition is taking place

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.370
in an area known as Low Earth Orbit.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:49.920
- It's first come, first serve.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.810
So they are racing to
occupy those orbital slots

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.990
because the ones that get there first

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.180
with the capability of presence

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.650
is going to be able to set the rules.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.810
- [Narrator] And that comes with privacy

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.200
and security concerns.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.800
Still, SpaceX is moving
ahead with the larger fleet

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.250
of satellites, while the
relative newcomer China

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.640
is betting on faster speeds

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.313
to build a powerful
internet network in space.

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:15.406
(dramatic music)

00:01:15.406 --> 00:01:17.936
Satellite-based internet
is actually not new.

00:01:17.936 --> 00:01:19.470
Since the mid-1990s,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.130
people who lived in remote areas

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.030
and don't have access to cell towers

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.160
have relied on satellites.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.280
These satellites are in
orbit about 22,000 miles away

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.890
from Earth, and a result of
that distance is slow speed.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.350
Because China's satellites
and the Starlink network

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.550
are closer to Earth,

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.810
that means they can provide
much faster services.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.290
SpaceX is currently promising
its beta test customers

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.460
in the US speeds between 50
and 150 megabits per second.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.850
That's still below the country's
average broadband service

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:48.683
on the ground.

00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:50.640
China has not rolled out its service yet

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.830
or demonstrated how its satellite works

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.620
but the university that
developed the 6G satellite

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.760
says it could offer speeds

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.027
that can potentially reach
at least terabyte per second.

00:02:00.027 --> 00:02:03.694
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.870
- [Narrator] That means if you
download a one-gigabyte movie

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.110
with Starlink's service,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.660
it would take about 10 seconds.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.270
But with the 6G speed that China promises,

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.061
you could download about
1,000 movies in just 1 second.

00:02:16.061 --> 00:02:19.978
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.430
- [Narrator] China says it's 6G plan

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.660
is an extension of its 5G network,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:26.870
which has already been rolled out

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.710
in many Chinese cities.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.180
This technological progression

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.266
is starting to help transform
the country's economy.

00:02:33.266 --> 00:02:34.430
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.170
- If you have 6G, your
ability to have automation

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.630
is going to improve exponentially.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.480
We mean better manufacturing.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.440
We mean better 3D printing.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.800
We mean better self-driving cars.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.970
- [Narrator] Namrata Goswami
is an independent researcher

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.760
who's been studying global space policy

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.270
for more than 20 years.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.430
And she says China's 6G satellite network

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.960
can make a big impact
once it's up and running.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.930
- Once you become the
first country to do it,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.500
you set the global standards,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.030
you set the rules in
terms of how this is going

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.090
to be regulated.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.610
Those who are first there

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.180
will be able to standardize the procedures

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.570
of that particular market.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:13.380
And then you also set the
innovation cycle in technology.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.260
- [Narrator] But SpaceX
has an edge over China

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.765
in another way.

00:03:16.765 --> 00:03:17.598
- [Announcer] And liftoff.

00:03:17.598 --> 00:03:18.431
(rocket whooshing)

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:19.970
- SpaceX has a big advantage

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.720
of having been the first mover.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.710
- [Narrator] Therese Jones
is a senior policy director

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.540
at the Satellite Industry Association.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.950
And she's been studying
satellite communication

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.490
of seven years.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.730
The Washington, D.C.
based group represents

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.390
over 50 satellite companies
in the US, including SpaceX.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:36.460
- The number is absolutely

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.140
a comparative advantage right now.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.450
It takes a long time to
ramp up the capabilities

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.180
to even get 1,500 satellites on orbit.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.200
And just ramp up your satellite
manufacturing capabilities

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.970
to reach that point.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.070
- [Narrator] That's because
SpaceX runs its own supply chain

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.520
to produce the satellites

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.660
and handles everything else

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.670
from launching to operating them.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.040
It's taken the company three years

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.100
to plot a constellation of
more than 1,000 satellites,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.350
planning to eventually
deploy a total of 12,000.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:07.570
- SpaceX has the goal of
providing ubiquitous connectivity

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:08.821
across the globe,

00:04:08.821 --> 00:04:12.420
whether that is providing
internet to rural areas,

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.270
whether it's providing
internet to ships, to planes.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:18.370
- [Narrator] And that global plan

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.500
could help more than three
billion people in the world

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.570
who don't have regular
access to the internet.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.020
- So the hardest-to-server customers.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:27.190
- [Narrator] After an installation cost

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.460
of about $600, the monthly fee

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.340
to use Starlink's satellites
would be about $100.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.420
SpaceX says it's already
received 500,000 pre-orders

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.760
for its service.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.360
And the company's plans for expansion

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.330
could get a boost from the US government

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.190
as it recently won preliminary rights

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:46.473
to nearly $900 million in subsidies.

00:04:46.473 --> 00:04:47.960
- [Announcer] Six, five.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:49.640
- [Narrator] But SpaceX's bids for funds

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.020
and it prolific launches

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.070
have started to draw criticism from rivals

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.570
in the US and Europe

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.080
who say Starlink's
satellites are crowding orbit

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.110
and endangering other satellites.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:00.630
So some are asking regulators

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.120
to clamp down on the SpaceX project.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.070
The company didn't respond
to a request for comment.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:07.810
China could also face similar pushback

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.310
from rivals since it also plans

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:11.680
to launch more than 10,000 satellites

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.316
but this mega plan has one strong ally.

00:05:14.316 --> 00:05:15.149
(people clapping)

00:05:15.149 --> 00:05:16.970
- The one advantage that the country has

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.410
is that it has one party.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:20.790
So the support is very much consistent.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:22.860
It's very clear, China's goal

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.303
under President Xi has been to
become a leader in innovation

00:05:26.303 --> 00:05:29.853
and technology and especially
communications technology.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.270
- [Narrator] But big networks
of satellites also generate

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.730
a lot of personal data.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:35.880
- There are these concerns

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.030
that your data can be turned over,

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.760
it could be used for espionage,
intelligence operations.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.723
It could be used to
have economic advantage

00:05:44.723 --> 00:05:48.150
because you can also spy
into their economic system.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.650
- [Narrator] For example, US officials

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:50.710
have long said Beijing

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.460
could direct Chinese telecom
companies like Huawei,

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.630
which have set up 5G networks abroad

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.410
to spy or sabotage.

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:58.690
Huawei and the Chinese government

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:00.170
have rejected the charges

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:02.670
but Goswami says China's 6G network

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.330
could open up similar concerns.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.060
- Chinese law makes it mandatory

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.620
for any company or citizen to collaborate

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:09.990
with Chinese intelligence

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.010
in case such a request is made.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:12.843
- [Narrator] But in the US,

00:06:12.843 --> 00:06:16.020
the legal system could
provide a level of protection.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.700
- SpaceX cannot turn over data

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.290
just because US intelligence will want it.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:23.270
You will have to go to
court to get permission.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.630
- [Narrator] SpaceX didn't
respond to a request for comment.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.150
While China's Foreign Ministry said

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.100
that Chinese law protects data security

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.074
and personal information of
citizens and organizations.

00:06:32.074 --> 00:06:34.210
(rocket booming)

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.000
For now, SpaceX is moving ahead

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.330
by seeking permission for
another 30,000 satellites

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.460
but China's network could
have one more advantage

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.380
when it's ready.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.010
- [Namrata] China can
always make it mandatory

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.850
for its citizen to have
that kind of service

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.360
in its devices.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.193
- [Narrator] Making the world's

00:06:49.193 --> 00:06:50.820
most populated country a place

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.540
to continue testing the service

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:53.572
and honing the technology.

00:06:53.572 --> 00:06:56.155
(lively music)

